# std/flake-builder's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3 — "Breaking the
# cycles"). flake-builder cannot be built by flake-builder, so its image is the
# sentinel `--base:docker=seeded`: `eval_expr_base` turns a `:docker=` ref
# into the blob `docker://seeded` with no dispatch (no re-entry into
# std/flake-builder), and the formed arg-tree is
# `{ base: docker://seeded, in: <this tree>, salt }`.
# Nothing ever pulls `docker://seeded`: the core-seeder-runner registered that
# exact key answers it with the pre-built flake-builder image (bootstrap's
# hand-built delta). If the seeder is absent the job hits the generic runner and
# fails loudly on a bogus image — an honest "bootstrap broken" signal, never a
# silent wrong answer.
#
# THE VALUE IS A CURRY binding `--required-caosd=true`, so every build — a
# caller's `run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder --in:@=.` — carries it
# without saying so: a curried base flattens into the job's arg tree. A build
# pushes its result (and its deps memo) to `caos-registry:5000`, a name that
# only resolves on the central host, so it must run on that host's caosd runner.
# The image a build PRODUCES is unaffected and runs anywhere.
#
# std/cargo is one such `run`, answered by the seeder: its seed record in
# stack/build-builtins.sh requires `required-caosd` for exactly this reason.
IMAGE=run --base:docker=seeded --in:@=. --lock:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake.lock --required-pool=seeded
curry --base=$IMAGE --required-caosd=true
